{"name":"Gen X Soft Club","title":"Gen X Soft Club","revision":233307,"url":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Gen_X_Soft_Club","retrieved":"2026-09-06","license":"CC BY-SA","licenseUrl":"https://www.fandom.com/licensing","language":"en","blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","text":"Gen X Soft Club was particularly popular in the United Kingdom at this time, succeeding the initial Britpop/Cool Britannia wave of the mid-1990s. Artists in the Electronica (Fatboy Slim, The Chemical Brothers, Stereolab, Massive Attack) and Post-Britpop (Radiohead, Travis, Starsailor, Doves) genres were seen as representing Y2K/GXSC in both sound and album covers."},{"kind":"heading","text":"History"},{"kind":"paragraph","text":"GXSC's popularity was a movement in futurist aesthetics that spawned from the economic recovery and growing technology industry of the mid-late 1990s. Some had criticisms about the overly optimistic, \"bubblegum\" styles of Y2K Futurism and McBling. A notable shift in the visual mood of these styles happened during the turn of the millennium, mainly among Gen Xers and older Millennials."},{"kind":"heading","text":"Comeback"},{"kind":"paragraph","text":"Today, the aesthetic is making a comeback as styles like Y2K Futurism and Frutiger Aero return to the public eye in the 2020s. Artists like George Clanton, CFCF, and Doss overlap a lot with GXSC and push forward with the style to this day."},{"kind":"heading","text":"Visuals"},{"kind":"paragraph","text":"GXSC mixes contemporary urban minimalism and lifestyle with Y2K Futurism elements, along with the heavy use of Lomo effect (an analog cross-processing method creating saturated, high-contrast, \"soft\"-looking photos), bleach bypass, and blur technics in photography, and natural and muted colors - especially greens, blues, beiges, tans, greys, and blacks. There are also elements of 1960s and 1970s nostalgia (Ultramodern Revival), which were most common with Generation X."},{"kind":"heading","text":"Fashion"},{"kind":"paragraph","text":"Following trends with the rest of the visual identity of the aesthetic, GXSC's fashion is inspired by Y2K Futurism's fashion but with a much more muted, softer appearance. Some fashion trends include nude lipstick, middle parts or natural textured hair, natural leathers, knee-high boots, duster jackets, and other alternative streetwear styles."},{"kind":"heading","text":"Media"},{"kind":"heading","text":"Movies"},{"kind":"list","text":"Chungking Express (1994)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Fallen Angels (1995)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Trainspotting (1996)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Gattaca (1997)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Happy Together (1997)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Nowhere (1997)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Sliding Doors (1998)"},{"kind":"list","text":"The Truman Show (1998)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Wonderland (1999)"},{"kind":"list","text":"The Beach (2000)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Code Unknown (2000)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Requiem for a Dream (2000)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Zoolander (2001)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Donnie Darko (2001)"},{"kind":"list","text":"The Hole (2001)"},{"kind":"list","text":"All About Lily Chou-Chou (2001)"},{"kind":"list","text":"A.I. Artificial Intelligence (2001)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Mulholland Drive (2001)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Millennium Mambo (2001)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Vanilla Sky (2001)"},{"kind":"list","text":"L.I.E. (2001)"},{"kind":"list","text":"The Ring (2002)"},{"kind":"list","text":"One Hour Photo (2002)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Simone (2002)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Resident Evil (2002)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Demon Love (2002)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Dark water (2002)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Thirteen (2003)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Lost in Translation (2003)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Garden State (2004)"},{"kind":"list","text":"The Butterfly Effect (2004)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless Mind (2004)"},{"kind":"list","text":"The Host (2006)"},{"kind":"list","text":"Ben X (2007)